# Cline Rules for Daily Report Engineering

## Project shape

- This is a Node.js 18+ application with a single HTTP server and a browser frontend.
- `server.js` owns routing, authentication, in-memory sessions, JSON persistence, and LINE notifications.
- `public/index.html`, `public/app.js`, and `public/styles.css` make up the frontend.
- `data/reports.json` and `data/users.json` are runtime data. Do not rewrite or seed them unless the task explicitly requires it.
- Read [README.md](README.md) for deployment, storage, and LINE configuration details.

## Working conventions

- Use the existing dependency-free Node.js and browser APIs unless a dependency is clearly necessary.
- Keep frontend behavior in `public/app.js`, presentation in `public/styles.css`, and API/domain behavior in `server.js`.
- Preserve the existing Thai user-facing language and current visual system when changing the UI.
- Keep edits focused; do not reformat unrelated code or change deployment configuration without a direct need.
- Never expose passwords, password hashes, salts, session tokens, or LINE credentials in responses, logs, screenshots, or committed files.

## Validation

- Start locally with `npm start` and use port `3030` unless `PORT` is set.
- Before finishing server changes, run `node --check server.js`; for frontend JavaScript, run `node --check public/app.js`.
- For API changes, verify unauthenticated requests, the intended authenticated role, persistence behavior, and error responses.
- Treat LINE notification failure as non-blocking: report creation must still succeed when LINE is unavailable or unconfigured.
- Do not claim tests passed unless they were actually run; this project currently has no test script.
